From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Paint declared widget depths with both selected output steps

Take the distances and width from the same complete Wayland event group.
Negative depths use the behind step and positive depths use the in-front
step, both converted at the selected output's pixel width.

Assisted-by: gpt-6.2-sol via Codex

diff --git a/src/3rdparty/wayland/protocols/kde-stereo-content/kde-stereo-content-v1.xml b/src/3rdparty/wayland/protocols/kde-stereo-content/kde-stereo-content-v1.xml
--- a/src/3rdparty/wayland/protocols/kde-stereo-content/kde-stereo-content-v1.xml
+++ b/src/3rdparty/wayland/protocols/kde-stereo-content/kde-stereo-content-v1.xml
@@ -151,11 +151,10 @@
     depth on the same screen. Before version 5 the client reads the user's
     settings itself.
 
-    Since version 7 the compositor also tells the client the user's element
-    limit (element_limit): how far an element drawn inside the surface may
-    lie in front of or behind what it lies on, a pixel or so, where the
-    depth limits above are for the desktop's own surfaces. A toolkit that
-    draws a surface's elements at their own depths takes its steps from it.
+    Since version 7 the compositor also sends the element steps for the
+    surface's selected output (element_limit). A toolkit uses these for
+    elements inside its surface. The desktop depth limits above apply to
+    the desktop's own surfaces.
 
     A surface may have any number of these objects. The depth state is sent
     to each of them. The surface is marked as desktop depth while any of
@@ -287,16 +286,15 @@
       value changes; depth_limits ends the group, and all three describe the
       same selected output.
 
-      limit is how far an element drawn inside the surface (a button, a
-      field, a page) may lie in front of or behind the surface it lies on,
-      in pixels of a view 1920 wide: the user's element limit, one step of
-      depth inside a window. Zero means the elements inside the surface lie
-      flat. The compositor gives it as the user set it; a client converts
-      it to its buffer's pixels as it converts the depth limits, through the
-      shared stereo_depth_limits_for_buffer conversion, and never rescales
-      it by the size of its window or of an element.
-    </description>
-    <arg name="limit" type="fixed" summary="the element limit, pixels of a view 1920 wide"/>
+      behind and in_front are the non-negative distances an element may lie
+      behind or in front of what it lies on. They are the user's element
+      limit multiplied by the selected output's sunk and popped factors,
+      respectively, in pixels of a view 1920 wide. Zero disables movement
+      in that direction. Clients convert the steps to buffer pixels the
+      same way as depth_limits, using the selected output width.
+    </description>
+    <arg name="behind" type="fixed" summary="distance behind, pixels of a view 1920 wide"/>
+    <arg name="in_front" type="fixed" summary="distance in front, pixels of a view 1920 wide"/>
   </event>
 
 </interface>
